  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x={y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-10y+25) & =0 \\\\ y(y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082348/assets/593d1168ce7328aa93edd61fcabc116451fcda8ef24faeac0949fa0a8e686b51.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x={y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=5.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\,({y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-10{\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+25{\\int }_{0}^{5}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}|}_{0}^{5}-{\\frac{10}{3}{y}^{3}|}_{0}^{5}+{\\frac{25}{2}{y}^{2}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4}-0)-\\frac{10}{3}({5}^{3}-0)+\\frac{25}{2}({5}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{1250}{3}+\\frac{625}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
